    "final_text": "I've built the todo CLI with all four commands, and all 81 tests pass under `npm test`. It needs no npm packages; the tests use Node's built-in test runner. I also ran the real CLI by hand in a scratch directory, and it behaved as expected, including the error cases.\n\n**Files**\n- `index.js`: the entry point. It passes the command-line arguments on and prints the result.\n- `lib/todos.js`: all the logic. `run()` returns the output and exit code instead of printing them, so tests can call it directly.\n- `package.json`: `\"test\": \"node --test\"`. It also registers a `todo` command in case you install it globally.\n- `test/todos.test.js`: 73 tests that call `run()` in a fresh temporary folder each time, with a fixed clock.\n- `test/cli.test.js`: 8 tests that run `node index.js` as a separate process to check the output streams and exit codes.\n\n**How it behaves**\n- `list` prints `1. [x] Buy groceries` style lines, with ids right-aligned, then a \"1 pending, 1 done\" summary. With no todos it prints \"No todos yet\u2026\".\n- Errors go to stderr as `Error: \u2026` with exit code 1; normal output goes to stdout with exit code 0. Running with no command prints usage and exits 1; `help`, `--help` or `-h` prints it and exits 0.\n- An id is rejected if it's missing, not a whole number (`abc`, `1.5`, `0x1`), zero or negative, or belongs to no todo. It's also rejected when the list is empty.\n- If `todos.json` is corrupt or in an unexpected shape, the command reports it and leaves the file untouched rather than overwriting it.\n- Saves write to a temporary file and then rename it over `todos.json`, so a crash mid-save can't leave a half-written file.\n\n**Choices I made where the spec was open**\n- **File format:** `todos.json` holds `{ \"nextId\": 3, \"todos\": [...] }` rather than a bare list. This keeps ids truly auto-incrementing: after you delete #2, the next todo is #3, not a reused #2. The app still reads a plain list if one is there.\n- **Adding without quotes:** `add Walk the dog` works the same as `add \"Walk the dog\"`. Leading and trailing spaces are trimmed, and blank text is rejected.\n- **Completing a finished todo:** it prints a notice and exits 0, not an error.\n- **Extra arguments:** `complete 1 2` is an error rather than silently acting on the first id.\n\nTo check the tests catch real bugs, I broke six behaviours in the code on purpose. The first run missed one: cleanup of the temporary file after a failed save. I added a test for it, and now all six are caught.\n\nNothing is committed; the files are untracked on `main`.",
